# Outbound HTTP adapters

Runtime: **httpx**, orjson. Prefer httpx (stack default). Mention aiohttp only if the target repo
already uses it — do not introduce aiohttp, and do not rewrite a working aiohttp adapter unasked.

New external HTTP API → a module under `project/infrastructure/adapters/`. Subclass or compose
`AsyncApi` (async) or `SyncApi` (sync) from `project/infrastructure/utils/base_client.py`. If that
helper is missing, copy sibling `CLIENT.md` into that path. `layers.toml`: `base_client` is
**adapters**, not presentation.

`name_for_monitoring` is required (API clients suffix `_api`). When the path is dynamic, pass
`resource_for_monitoring` as a template with no IDs (`invoices/{invoice_id}`, not `invoices/123`).

## Errors

Map in `error_handling` so domain code never sees httpx (or aiohttp) exceptions (`python-exceptions`):

| Failure | Raise |
|---|---|
| 4xx | `ClientError` |
| 5xx | `ServerError` |
| Other HTTP status | `ExternalApiError` |
| Transport / timeout | `ExternalHTTPConnectionError` |

Per-adapter subclasses of those types are optional. Override `error_handling` / `process_response` /
`response_to_native` when the API needs extra mapping. HTTP status is `response.status_code`.

## Session and JSON

Reuse connections with `async with client.api.Session():` (or `with` for `SyncApi`). Encode JSON with
orjson. Do not call `raise_for_status` — status mapping lives in `error_handling`.

```python
class InvoicesClient:
    def __init__(self, token: str) -> None:
        self.api = AsyncApi(
            "https://api.example.com",
            name_for_monitoring="invoices_api",
            headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {token}"},
        )

    async def get_invoice(self, invoice_id: str) -> dict:
        return await self.api.call_endpoint(
            f"invoices/{invoice_id}",
            resource_for_monitoring="invoices/{invoice_id}",
        )
```

## Monitoring

When `python-monitoring` is installed, `AsyncApi` / `SyncApi` record HTTP metrics (`is_build_metrics` /
`http_tracking`). Do not also set `ClientSession` to `HttpxClientWithMonitoring` on the same client
(double-count). Use `HttpxClientWithMonitoring` only when you need a tracked httpx client **without**
this AppError mapping. Guard metric imports — do not require `llm_common` when monitoring is absent.

## Tests

Mock outbound calls with `httpx_responses` (`python-tests`). Do not `patch` httpx or the adapter.
Stub the adapter in use-case tests via `Container.local(...)`.
